1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,277
This is STAT 331, video 8. The topic is hypothesis tests and p-values for the slope beta 1.

2
00:00:08,727 --> 00:00:15,191
This video prepares you for four question types. Each type gets a method card and worked problems.

3
00:00:15,641 --> 00:00:27,225
Type A: test the null hypothesis beta 1 equals theta 0 against a two-sided alternative. The slides, two tutorials and Assignment 1 ask it.

4
00:00:27,675 --> 00:00:32,241
Type B: a one-sided test, when the question names one direction.

5
00:00:32,691 --> 00:00:42,013
Type C: explain a test result. What a p-value means, how a test is linked to the confidence interval, and the type one error rate.

6
00:00:42,463 --> 00:00:47,306
Type D: simulate p-values, as in Assignment 1, question 4.

7
00:00:47,756 --> 00:01:07,489
Section 1 builds the tool that all four question types use. We test the null hypothesis H 0: beta 1 equals theta 0, against H 1: beta 1 is not equal to theta 0. Theta 0 is a fixed number from the question. Often it is 0.

8
00:01:07,939 --> 00:01:20,419
Suppose H 0 is true. Then beta 1 hat is normal, with mean theta 0 and variance sigma squared over S x x. The purple curve shows this distribution.

9
00:01:20,869 --> 00:01:26,075
An estimate near theta 0 is not surprising if H 0 is true.

10
00:01:26,525 --> 00:01:34,887
An estimate far out in a tail is surprising if H 0 is true. Such an estimate is evidence against H 0.

11
00:01:35,337 --> 00:01:43,401
So a test measures one thing. How extreme are our data, if H 0 is true? Slide 41 gives this goal.

12
00:01:43,851 --> 00:02:00,406
Where does the test statistic come from? Line 1, from video 6: beta 1 hat is normal, with mean beta 1 and variance sigma squared over S x x. The arrow is its standard deviation, sigma over root S x x.

13
00:02:00,856 --> 00:02:14,403
Line 2: subtract the mean, beta 1, and divide by the standard deviation. The result is standard normal, with mean 0 and standard deviation 1. The marked sigma is unknown.

14
00:02:14,853 --> 00:02:28,378
Line 3: put sigma hat in place of sigma, as in video 7. Then the ratio has a t distribution with n minus 2 degrees of freedom. Its tails are wider than the normal tails.

15
00:02:28,828 --> 00:02:44,423
Line 4: under H 0, beta 1 equals theta 0. So put theta 0 in place of the marked beta 1. The numerator, beta 1 hat minus theta 0, is the distance on the beta 1 scale.

16
00:02:44,873 --> 00:02:57,267
Line 5: the marked denominator, sigma hat over root S x x, is the standard error of beta 1 hat. In the picture, the distance is two and a half standard errors.

17
00:02:57,717 --> 00:03:13,013
So T counts the standard errors from theta 0 to beta 1 hat. Here t equals 2.5. T uses only the data and theta 0, so we can compute it. Question type A uses it in step 2.

18
00:03:13,463 --> 00:03:34,989
The p-value answers one question. If H 0 is true, what is the probability of a test statistic as extreme as the observed one, or more extreme? The example on the right has 10 degrees of freedom and t observed 2.1. More extreme means farther from 0: the two orange tails.

19
00:03:35,439 --> 00:03:48,068
Line 2: the two tails are two separate events. T is at least 2.1, or T is at most minus 2.1. Each tail has an area of 0.031.

20
00:03:48,518 --> 00:03:56,177
Line 3: the t curve is symmetric, so the two tails are equal. The p-value is two times the right tail.

21
00:03:56,627 --> 00:04:06,440
Line 4: the right tail is 1 minus the area to the left of 2.1. That area, in blue, is 0.969.

22
00:04:06,890 --> 00:04:21,098
So p equals 2 times 1 minus 0.969. That is 2 times 0.031, which is 0.062. Question type A uses this in step 4.

23
00:04:21,548 --> 00:04:34,497
Now the decision. Choose a level alpha before you look at the data. Often alpha is 0.05. Slide 43 rejects H 0 when the p-value is below alpha.

24
00:04:34,947 --> 00:04:41,987
Put in the p-value from the last slide: 2 times P of T at least the absolute value of t observed.

25
00:04:42,437 --> 00:04:52,165
Divide by 2. The right tail beyond the absolute t must be smaller than alpha over 2. Each red tail has area alpha over 2.

26
00:04:52,615 --> 00:05:13,394
The tail to the right of t 1 minus alpha over 2, with n minus 2 degrees of freedom, has area exactly alpha over 2. A smaller tail starts farther right. So reject when the absolute value of t is above this critical value. With 10 degrees of freedom, it is 2.228.

27
00:05:13,844 --> 00:05:28,095
Check with t equal to 2.1. It is inside the critical values, and its p-value, 0.062, is above 0.05. Both rules say: do not reject.

28
00:05:28,545 --> 00:05:45,931
Now t equal to 3.0. It is in a tail, and its p-value, 0.013, is below 0.05. Both rules say: reject. The two rules always agree. Question type A uses them in step 4.

29
00:05:46,381 --> 00:06:01,827
Section 2. Question type A: test H 0, beta 1 equals theta 0, against a two-sided alternative. The slides do it for the brain data. Tutorial 1, Tutorial 2 and Assignment 1 ask it.

30
00:06:02,277 --> 00:06:12,730
Method card A has five steps. Step 1: write the hypotheses. Take theta 0 from the question, in the units of y per unit of x.

31
00:06:13,180 --> 00:06:28,924
Step 2: compute t observed. It is beta 1 hat minus theta 0, over the standard error. The standard error is sigma hat over root S x x, or read it from the output. The blue line marks t observed.

32
00:06:29,374 --> 00:06:42,089
Step 3: the reference distribution is t with n minus 2 degrees of freedom. Find the critical value, t 1 minus alpha over 2, n minus 2. The red tails start there.

33
00:06:42,539 --> 00:06:55,787
Step 4: reject H 0 if the absolute value of t observed is above the critical value. This is the same as a p-value below alpha. The orange tails are the p-value.

34
00:06:56,237 --> 00:07:06,797
Step 5: conclude in words, with the variables of the question. When you do not reject, say: the data do not give enough evidence against H 0.

35
00:07:07,247 --> 00:07:26,447
Here is the example of the slides. Pause the video and try it. 236 adults, brain weight y in grams and head size x in cubic centimeters. Step 1 of card A: H 0, beta 1 equals 0, against beta 1 not equal to 0.

36
00:07:26,897 --> 00:07:51,409
Step 2: beta 1 hat is 0.26082, and its standard error is 0.01307. t observed is 0.26082 over 0.01307, which is 19.96. R prints 19.957, because it uses more digits.

37
00:07:51,859 --> 00:08:06,131
Step 3: there are 234 degrees of freedom: 236 minus 2. The critical value t 0.975, 234 is 1.970.

38
00:08:06,581 --> 00:08:12,127
Step 4: 19.96 is far above 1.970.

39
00:08:12,577 --> 00:08:26,423
The p-value is 2 times P of T at least 19.957. Slide 43 gives 2.03 times 10 to the minus 52. It is below 0.05.

40
00:08:26,873 --> 00:08:37,582
Step 5: reject H 0 at the 5 percent level. The data give strong evidence of a linear association between head size and brain weight.

41
00:08:38,032 --> 00:08:48,208
Slide 45 gives the R output for the same fit. You do not need the code, but you must read the numbers. Each row is one coefficient.

42
00:08:48,658 --> 00:09:03,847
Card A, step 2. In the x row, the Estimate column gives beta 1 hat, 0.26082. The Std. Error column gives its standard error, 0.01307.

43
00:09:04,297 --> 00:09:17,759
The t value column is the estimate over the standard error: 19.957. It counts the standard errors from 0 to beta 1 hat. The ruler shows about 20 of them.

44
00:09:18,209 --> 00:09:30,966
Step 4. The last column is the two-sided p-value for H 0, beta 1 equals 0. Less than 2 times 10 to the minus 16 means: too small to print.

45
00:09:31,416 --> 00:09:41,208
The table always uses theta 0 equal to 0. For another theta 0, compute t observed yourself, with step 2 of card A.

46
00:09:41,658 --> 00:09:55,098
The intercept row tests H 0, beta 0 equals 0, in the same way. 335.43 over 47.78 is 7.021.

47
00:09:55,548 --> 00:10:11,377
Step 3 needs the degrees of freedom. The last line gives sigma hat, 72.35, on 234 degrees of freedom. 234 is n minus 2, so n is 236.

48
00:10:11,827 --> 00:10:32,670
Now Tutorial 1, question 2 c. Pause the video and try it. Test the significance of beta 1 at the 5 percent level. Significance of beta 1 means theta 0 equals 0. Step 1: H 0, beta 1 equals 0, against beta 1 not equal to 0.

49
00:10:33,120 --> 00:10:53,173
Step 2: the hours row gives beta 1 hat, 6.410, and its standard error, 0.499. t observed is 6.410 minus 0, over 0.499, which is 12.85. The output prints 12.8.

50
00:10:53,623 --> 00:11:06,530
Step 3: the output gives 98 degrees of freedom, 100 minus 2. The critical value t 0.975, 98 is 1.984.

51
00:11:06,980 --> 00:11:20,143
Step 4: 12.85 is far above 1.984. The p-value in the last column is less than 2 times 10 to the minus 16. It is below 0.05.

52
00:11:20,593 --> 00:11:31,622
Step 5: reject H 0 at the 5 percent level. The data give strong evidence that the mean maintenance cost changes with the number of flight hours.

53
00:11:32,072 --> 00:11:48,627
Part d. Pause the video and try it. Step 1: y is in thousands of dollars. So 6000 dollars is 6 thousand, and theta 0 is 6. H 0: beta 1 equals 6, against beta 1 not equal to 6.

54
00:11:49,077 --> 00:12:10,325
Step 2: t observed is 6.410 minus 6, over 0.499. That is 0.41 over 0.499, which is 0.82. The t value in the output uses theta 0 equal to 0, so compute this t yourself.

55
00:12:10,775 --> 00:12:17,537
Step 3: 98 degrees of freedom again. The critical value is 1.984.

56
00:12:17,987 --> 00:12:25,795
Step 4: 0.82 is inside the critical values, plus and minus 1.984.

57
00:12:26,245 --> 00:12:38,277
The p-value is 2 times P of T at least 0.82, with 98 degrees of freedom. It is 0.413, above 0.05.

58
00:12:38,727 --> 00:12:50,631
Step 5: do not reject H 0 at the 5 percent level. The data do not give enough evidence against an average increase of 6000 dollars per flight hour.

59
00:12:51,081 --> 00:13:10,153
A type A question from Tutorial 2, question 1 b. Pause the video and try it. Does satisfaction depend on severity? This asks if beta 1 is different from 0. Step 1: H 0, beta 1 equals 0, against beta 1 not equal to 0.

60
00:13:10,603 --> 00:13:33,046
Step 2: the Severity row gives the estimate, minus 2.3283, and the standard error, 0.5859. t observed is minus 2.3283 over 0.5859, which is minus 3.974. The t value column shows the same number.

61
00:13:33,496 --> 00:13:46,275
Step 3: the output gives 23 degrees of freedom, 25 minus 2. The critical value t 0.975, 23 is 2.069.

62
00:13:46,725 --> 00:14:04,623
Step 4: t is negative, so use its absolute value, 3.974. It is above 2.069. The last column gives the p-value, 0.000601. It is below 0.05.

63
00:14:05,073 --> 00:14:17,276
Step 5: reject H 0. Satisfaction depends on severity. The estimate is negative, so the estimated mean satisfaction decreases as severity increases.

64
00:14:17,726 --> 00:14:30,910
The last type A problem is Assignment 1, question 3 c. Pause the video and try it. Step 1: H 0, beta 1 equals 0, against beta 1 not equal to 0.

65
00:14:31,360 --> 00:14:58,752
Step 2 needs the standard error. Sigma hat is 0.9178 and S x x is 70657.4. The square root of 70657.4 is 265.81. 0.9178 over 265.81 is 0.003453.

66
00:14:59,202 --> 00:15:10,978
Then t observed is minus 0.02280 over 0.003453, which is minus 6.603.

67
00:15:11,428 --> 00:15:22,585
Step 3: 88 degrees of freedom, 90 minus 2. The critical value t 0.975, 88 is 1.987.

68
00:15:23,035 --> 00:15:38,715
Step 4: the absolute value, 6.603, is above 1.987. The p-value, 2 times P of T at least 6.603, is 2.95 times 10 to the minus 9.

69
00:15:39,165 --> 00:15:57,896
Step 5: reject H 0 at the 0.05 level. The data give strong evidence of a linear association. The estimated mean chewiness decreases as sugar increases. Report t equals minus 6.603 and the p-value.

70
00:15:58,346 --> 00:16:12,383
Section 3. So far the alternative was two-sided. Then both tails count. For t observed 2.1 with 10 degrees of freedom, the two-sided p-value is 0.062.

71
00:16:12,833 --> 00:16:20,705
Sometimes the question names one direction. For example, H 1: beta 1 is greater than theta 0.

72
00:16:21,155 --> 00:16:29,177
Slide 49: then only large values of beta 1 hat minus theta 0 are evidence for H 1.

73
00:16:29,627 --> 00:16:37,136
Divide by the standard error, which is positive. So only large values of T are evidence for H 1.

74
00:16:37,586 --> 00:16:47,741
So the p-value has one tail only: P of T at least t observed. Here it is 0.031, half of the two-sided value.

75
00:16:48,191 --> 00:17:03,551
When t observed is positive, the one-sided p-value is half of the two-sided p-value. When t observed is negative, it is above one half, and we do not reject. Question type B uses this in step 3.

76
00:17:04,001 --> 00:17:08,886
Method card B solves every one-sided test. It has five steps.

77
00:17:09,336 --> 00:17:23,672
Step 1: read the direction in the question. Increases, or more than, gives H 1: beta 1 greater than theta 0. Decreases, or less than, gives H 1: beta 1 less than theta 0.

78
00:17:24,122 --> 00:17:29,050
Step 2: compute t observed, as in card A. The blue line marks it.

79
00:17:29,500 --> 00:17:45,436
Step 3: the p-value has one tail, in the direction of H 1. For greater than, it is P of T at least t observed. For less than, it is P of T at most t observed. Here it is the orange tail, 0.031.

80
00:17:45,886 --> 00:17:56,275
Step 4: reject H 0 if p is below alpha. Here 0.031 is below 0.05, so we reject.

81
00:17:56,725 --> 00:18:00,949
Step 5: conclude in words, with the direction of the question.

82
00:18:01,399 --> 00:18:17,463
A type B question on the Tutorial 1 output. Pause the video and try it. Does 1 more flight hour increase the mean cost by more than 6000 dollars? Step 1: more than gives H 1: beta 1 greater than 6.

83
00:18:17,913 --> 00:18:28,857
Step 2: t observed is 6.410 minus 6, over 0.499, which is 0.82, as in part d.

84
00:18:29,307 --> 00:18:43,473
Step 3: one tail, to the right: P of T at least 0.82, with 98 degrees of freedom. It is 0.207, half of the two-sided 0.413.

85
00:18:43,923 --> 00:18:52,093
Step 4: 0.207 is above 0.05, so we do not reject H 0.

86
00:18:52,543 --> 00:19:01,183
Step 5: the data do not give enough evidence that the mean cost increases by more than 6000 dollars per flight hour.

87
00:19:01,633 --> 00:19:16,076
A type B question on the Tutorial 2 output. Pause the video and try it. Does the mean satisfaction decrease as severity increases? Step 1: decrease gives H 1: beta 1 less than 0.

88
00:19:16,526 --> 00:19:21,987
Step 2: the output gives t observed, minus 3.974.

89
00:19:22,437 --> 00:19:39,483
Step 3: one tail, to the left, the direction of H 1. The output gives the two-sided p-value, 0.000601. The left tail is half of it: 0.000300.

90
00:19:39,933 --> 00:19:49,191
Step 4: 0.000300 is below 0.05, so we reject H 0.

91
00:19:49,641 --> 00:19:56,895
Step 5: the data give strong evidence that the mean satisfaction decreases as severity increases.

92
00:19:57,345 --> 00:20:04,726
Section 4. Question type C asks you to explain a test result. Method card C has four steps.

93
00:20:05,176 --> 00:20:11,149
Step 1: say what the p-value measures. It starts from an assumption: H 0 is true.

94
00:20:11,599 --> 00:20:17,594
Then T has a known curve: the t distribution with n minus 2 degrees of freedom.

95
00:20:18,044 --> 00:20:35,260
The p-value is the area in the tails beyond the observed t. So the p-value is a probability about the data, computed under H 0. Beta 1 is a fixed, unknown constant. Slides 46 and 47 give this meaning.

96
00:20:35,710 --> 00:20:50,451
Step 2: compare p with alpha. If p is below alpha, reject H 0: the data give evidence against H 0. If not, do not reject: the data do not give enough evidence against H 0.

97
00:20:50,901 --> 00:21:08,608
Step 3, for a question about the confidence interval. A two-sided test at level alpha rejects theta 0 exactly when theta 0 is outside the 100 times 1 minus alpha percent confidence interval. The next slide derives it.

98
00:21:09,058 --> 00:21:21,730
Step 4, for a question about error rates. If H 0 is true, the rule p below alpha rejects in a fraction alpha of repeated samples. This is the type one error rate.

99
00:21:22,180 --> 00:21:39,375
Here is the reason for step 3 of card C. Line 1: from card A, reject H 0 when the absolute value of t observed is above t star. t star is t 1 minus alpha over 2, with n minus 2 degrees of freedom.

100
00:21:39,825 --> 00:21:47,718
Line 2: write t observed. It is the distance from theta 0 to beta 1 hat, over the standard error.

101
00:21:48,168 --> 00:21:59,325
Line 3: multiply both sides by the standard error, which is positive. Now the distance is compared with t star standard errors, the green half-width.

102
00:21:59,775 --> 00:22:15,583
Line 4: open the absolute value. Theta 0 is more than t star standard errors below beta 1 hat, or more than t star standard errors above it. In the picture, theta 0 is outside the green band.

103
00:22:16,033 --> 00:22:32,140
Line 5: the green band, beta 1 hat plus or minus t star standard errors, is the 100 times 1 minus alpha percent confidence interval of video 7. So theta 0 is outside the interval.

104
00:22:32,590 --> 00:22:48,377
The result: a two-sided test at level alpha rejects theta 0 exactly when theta 0 is outside the 100 times 1 minus alpha percent confidence interval. Question type C uses it in step 3.

105
00:22:48,827 --> 00:23:11,120
Assignment 1, question 2 a. Pause the video and try it. We reject H 0, beta 1 equals 0, at the 0.05 level. What does this say about the 95 percent confidence interval? This is step 3 of card C, with theta 0 equal to 0 and alpha equal to 0.05.

106
00:23:11,570 --> 00:23:23,410
First, a case where we do not reject. The top line is the t scale. t equal to 1.4 is inside the green region, between minus t star and t star.

107
00:23:23,860 --> 00:23:38,345
The bottom line is the beta 1 scale. Beta 1 hat is 1.4 standard errors from 0. The half-width of the interval is t star standard errors, which is more. So the interval contains 0.

108
00:23:38,795 --> 00:23:44,470
Now a case where we reject. t equal to 3.2 is outside the green region.

109
00:23:44,920 --> 00:23:53,965
Beta 1 hat is now 3.2 standard errors from 0, more than the half-width. So the interval does not contain 0.

110
00:23:54,415 --> 00:24:07,279
The answer: the 95 percent confidence interval for beta 1 does not contain 0. The reason is the chain on the last slide, with theta 0 equal to 0.

111
00:24:07,729 --> 00:24:32,860
Check card C, step 3, with Tutorial 1. The 95 percent confidence interval is 6.410 plus or minus 1.984 times 0.499. 1.984 times 0.499 is 0.990. So the interval is from 5.42 to 7.40.

112
00:24:33,310 --> 00:24:40,734
0 is outside the interval. This matches part c, where the test rejected beta 1 equals 0.

113
00:24:41,184 --> 00:24:48,779
6 is inside the interval. This matches part d, where the test did not reject beta 1 equals 6.

114
00:24:49,229 --> 00:25:08,194
Slide 44 asks what to say when the p-value is above 0.05. Our own numbers: beta 1 hat is 0.4, the standard error is 0.3, with 20 degrees of freedom. t is 1.33, and p is 0.20.

115
00:25:08,644 --> 00:25:27,161
The 95 percent confidence interval is 0.4 plus or minus 2.086 times 0.3. That is 0.4 plus or minus 0.626, from minus 0.23 to 1.03.

116
00:25:27,611 --> 00:25:33,606
0 is inside the interval, so the test does not reject beta 1 equals 0.

117
00:25:34,056 --> 00:25:41,544
But 0.5 and 0.9 are also inside. The data agree with all of these values.

118
00:25:41,994 --> 00:25:57,973
So the conclusion of card C, step 2: do not reject H 0. The data do not give enough evidence against beta 1 equals 0. Slide 44 says it in the same way: we do not have enough evidence to reject.

119
00:25:58,423 --> 00:26:12,140
Card C, step 4. Use the rule: reject H 0 when p is below 5 percent. Suppose H 0 is true, and we repeat the experiment many times. Each time we compute t.

120
00:26:12,590 --> 00:26:27,310
Each dot is one simulated experiment with 10 points, where the true beta 1 is 0. Here are the first 20. Most t values are between the critical values, plus and minus 2.306.

121
00:26:27,760 --> 00:26:29,936
Here are 20 more experiments.

122
00:26:30,386 --> 00:26:40,861
2 of the 40 t values fall in the red regions. In those experiments we reject H 0, but H 0 is true. This is a type one error.

123
00:26:41,311 --> 00:26:55,625
In the long run, the rule p below 0.05 rejects a true H 0 in 5 percent of the experiments. This is the type one error rate. Here 2 out of 40 is 5 percent.

124
00:26:56,075 --> 00:27:06,166
Section 5. Question type D asks you to simulate p-values, as in Assignment 1, question 4. Method card D has six steps.

125
00:27:06,616 --> 00:27:21,336
Step 1: fix the x values and the true beta 0, beta 1 and sigma. Simulate each y i from a normal distribution with mean beta 0 plus beta 1 x i and variance sigma squared.

126
00:27:21,786 --> 00:27:29,338
Step 2: fit least squares to the simulated data. Find beta 1 hat, sigma hat, and the standard error.

127
00:27:29,788 --> 00:27:36,999
Step 3: compute t observed and the two-sided p-value for H 0, beta 1 equals 0.

128
00:27:37,449 --> 00:27:44,851
Step 4: repeat steps 1 to 3 many times, for example 2000 times. Store each p-value.

129
00:27:45,301 --> 00:27:53,664
Step 5: draw a histogram of the p-values. The share of p-values below 0.05 is the rejection rate.

130
00:27:54,114 --> 00:28:07,575
Step 6: explain the rate. If H 0 is true, it is close to alpha. If beta 1 is not 0, the rate grows with beta 1 over the standard deviation of beta 1 hat.

131
00:28:08,025 --> 00:28:29,316
Assignment 1, question 4 a. Pause the video and try it. The true model has 10 fixed x values and beta 0 equal to 1. Step 1 of card D: take beta 1 equal to 0.25 and sigma equal to 1. The gray line is the true mean, 1 plus 0.25 x.

132
00:28:29,766 --> 00:28:41,265
Simulate one y for each x, from a normal distribution with this mean and standard deviation 1. The table and the blue points show one simulated data set.

133
00:28:41,715 --> 00:28:52,616
Step 2: fit least squares. First S x x. x bar is 5. The last row holds x i minus 5, squared. These add to 76.

134
00:28:53,066 --> 00:29:04,266
The fit gives beta 1 hat equal to 0.1946 and sigma hat equal to 0.9885. The green line is the fitted line.

135
00:29:04,716 --> 00:29:22,636
Step 3: the standard error is 0.9885 over root 76, which is 0.1134. t observed is 0.1946 over 0.1134, which is 1.716.

136
00:29:23,086 --> 00:29:34,798
There are 10 minus 2, so 8 degrees of freedom. The two-sided p-value, 2 times P of T at least 1.716, is 0.124.

137
00:29:35,248 --> 00:29:54,149
Step 4: store this p-value, and repeat 2000 times. This data set does not reject, but the true beta 1 is 0.25. The assignment does this in R. The video uses Python with its own random numbers, so your numbers differ a little.

138
00:29:54,599 --> 00:30:09,746
Part b: beta 1 equals 0.25 and sigma equals 1. Step 5 of card D: the histogram of the 2000 p-values. The red bar holds the p-values below 0.05.

139
00:30:10,196 --> 00:30:26,367
975 of the 2000 p-values are below 0.05. So we reject in 48.75 percent of the samples. In about half of the samples, the test does not detect the true slope.

140
00:30:26,817 --> 00:30:31,339
Part c: now beta 1 equals 0, so H 0 is true.

141
00:30:31,789 --> 00:30:49,517
The p-values spread evenly from 0 to 1, and the histogram is flat. 98 of 2000 are below 0.05: 4.9 percent. This is close to 5 percent, the type one error rate of card C, step 4.

142
00:30:49,967 --> 00:31:01,530
Part d repeats parts b and c with a smaller error standard deviation, sigma equals 0.5. First, beta 1 equals 0.25.

143
00:31:01,980 --> 00:31:13,436
Almost all p-values are below 0.05: 1944 of 2000. We reject in 97.2 percent of the samples.

144
00:31:13,886 --> 00:31:17,982
Now beta 1 equals 0, so H 0 is true again.

145
00:31:18,432 --> 00:31:35,179
The histogram is flat again. 82 of 2000 are below 0.05: 4.1 percent, about 5 percent. With 2000 samples, values from about 4 to 6 percent are normal random variation.

146
00:31:35,629 --> 00:31:51,991
Part e asks you to explain. Step 6 of card D. The standard deviation of beta 1 hat is sigma over root S x x. Here S x x is 76, and root 76 is 8.718.

147
00:31:52,441 --> 00:32:13,583
With sigma equal to 1, the true slope 0.25 is 0.25 times 8.718, which is 2.18 standard deviations from 0. The simulated t values center near 2.18. About half of them pass the critical value 2.306.

148
00:32:14,033 --> 00:32:28,497
With sigma equal to 0.5, the standard deviation is half as large. The true slope is 4.36 standard deviations from 0. Almost all t values pass the critical value.

149
00:32:28,947 --> 00:32:38,355
When H 0 is true, T has a t distribution with 8 degrees of freedom, for every sigma. So the rate is about 5 percent.

150
00:32:38,805 --> 00:32:56,170
So the answer: when H 0 is true, we reject about 5 percent of the time, for every sigma. When beta 1 is not 0, a smaller sigma makes beta 1 hat more precise. Then the absolute t is larger, and we reject more often.

151
00:32:56,620 --> 00:33:13,281
To recap. Card A tests beta 1 equals theta 0 against a two-sided alternative. Write the hypotheses, compute t observed, find the critical value with n minus 2 degrees of freedom, compare, and conclude in words.

152
00:33:13,731 --> 00:33:21,561
Card B is the one-sided test. The direction in the question gives H 1, and the p-value has one tail.

153
00:33:22,011 --> 00:33:40,613
Card C explains a result. The p-value is computed under H 0. Not rejecting means not enough evidence. A two-sided test rejects theta 0 exactly when theta 0 is outside the confidence interval. The type one error rate is alpha.

154
00:33:41,063 --> 00:33:53,821
Card D simulates p-values. When H 0 is true, about 5 percent are below 0.05. The next video estimates the mean response at a given x.
